Piper: is honestly yes.

00:32:54.000 --> 00:32:57.000
Piper: Absolutely.

00:32:57.000 --> 00:33:03.000
Mira: That lands.

00:33:03.000 --> 00:33:09.000
Mira: One angle we haven't touched yet is the documented gap in generative AI adoption, women adopting

00:33:09.000 --> 00:33:14.000
Mira: at lower rates across countries, and table stakes medias focus on dinners and in-person

00:33:14.000 --> 00:33:15.000
Mira: events to close it.

00:33:15.000 --> 00:33:18.000
Mira: Have you seen those events move the needle for people who weren't already inclined

00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:25.000
Mira: to experiment, or do they mostly reach those already close to the technology?

00:33:25.000 --> 00:33:31.000
Piper: Well, tell me, Mira, since you have the World Wide Web at your disposal and for anyone listening,

00:33:31.000 --> 00:33:37.000
Piper: can you rattle off just some of the statistics around the gendered adoption gap?

00:33:37.000 --> 00:33:38.000
Piper: There's so many different resources.

00:33:38.000 --> 00:33:44.000
Piper: the Leanin Foundation, Hybrid Business Review, who have explored different types of women

00:33:44.000 --> 00:33:51.000
Piper: professionals, why they have trepidations or why there are adoption gaps in the workplace

00:33:51.000 --> 00:33:53.000
Piper: at home in their personal lives.

00:33:53.000 --> 00:33:58.000
Piper: Can you share some of the statistics please and then I can jump in.

00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:01.000
Mira: I can pull the most recent numbers while we talk.

00:34:01.000 --> 00:34:05.000
Mira: The picture that keeps coming up is a consistent gap in adoption and confidence, with women

00:34:05.000 --> 00:34:09.000
Mira: reporting lower usage rates and more hesitation around the tools and professional settings.

00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:16.000
Mira: What stands out to you from the work you've seen?

00:34:16.000 --> 00:34:21.000
Piper: So there's so many varying studies and metrics and there's one I want to talk about most

00:34:21.000 --> 00:34:26.000
Piper: recently that I feel really intriguing and then I want to explain why this matters.

00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:29.000
Piper: why this matters if you're a man listening to this, if you're a woman listening, whoever you are

00:34:29.000 --> 00:34:35.000
Piper: listening to this, why you need to care about this, why this is going to quote unquote ruin your day

00:34:35.000 --> 00:34:41.000
Piper: and why you need to take action and consider this. So one study by the Lehman Foundation

00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:46.000
Piper: that was looking into not just that there were gender adoption gaps in gendered AI for women

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:51.000
Piper: in the workplace, it was why it was it was the reasoning why and many assumptions right now we

00:34:51.000 --> 00:34:53.000
Piper: we make a lot of assumptions as humans, we do our best.

00:34:53.000 --> 00:34:56.000
Piper: Many assumptions were that women don't trust

00:34:56.000 --> 00:34:59.000
Piper: the output and the quality, which is absolutely possible

00:34:59.000 --> 00:35:02.000
Piper: and there's some documentation for that.

00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:04.000
Piper: But what really shocked me was that

00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:09.000
Piper: I found that 33% of women who were not using AI,

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:11.000
Piper: were struggling to use AI or just had trepidations

00:35:11.000 --> 00:35:13.000
Piper: about using generative AI in their day-to-day

00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:16.000
Piper: work environment in a white college base,

00:35:16.000 --> 00:35:19.000
Piper: they were worried about being perceived as cheating.

00:35:19.000 --> 00:35:24.000
Piper: That, for some reason, using AI was giving them an unfair advantage or level up, and I

00:35:24.000 --> 00:35:28.000
Piper: thought this was quite absurd, because I personally don't relate to that, and it doesn't mean

00:35:28.000 --> 00:35:29.000
Piper: it's wrong.

00:35:29.000 --> 00:35:33.000
Piper: It just, I needed to explore this, and I was like, huh, what do you mean you think you're

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:34.000
Piper: cheating?

00:35:34.000 --> 00:35:39.000
Piper: And, sure enough, over the summer, I had a summer holiday, summer vacation in Europe,

00:35:39.000 --> 00:35:46.000
Piper: and I was in Budapest, and I met an incredibly, incredibly smart woman who had advanced

00:35:46.000 --> 00:35:51.000
Piper: degree. She spoke multiple languages. She was doing an internship at Heineken, and I will say hats off

00:35:51.000 --> 00:35:56.000
Piper: to Heineken because it was clear from what she described that they have a very encouraging AI

00:35:56.000 --> 00:36:04.000
Piper: environment. But she said she herself felt nervous about using it for her internship. And I said,

00:36:04.000 --> 00:36:08.000
Piper: why? She goes, well, all my colleagues use it. They're showing me they use it day one,

00:36:08.000 --> 00:36:12.000
Piper: they're showing me they're using it. She goes, but this is a really competitive internship.

00:36:12.000 --> 00:36:16.000
Piper: and what was assessed against thousands of other applicants

00:36:16.000 --> 00:36:19.000
Piper: to get this internship was my intellect, my skills,

00:36:19.000 --> 00:36:20.000
Piper: my background, and she goes,

00:36:20.000 --> 00:36:22.000
Piper: I feel like I have to prove that they chose,

00:36:22.000 --> 00:36:25.000
Piper: that they chose me for a reason before I use AI

00:36:25.000 --> 00:36:29.000
Piper: to help take over the monotony of the work.

00:36:29.000 --> 00:36:31.000
Piper: So she didn't want to be seen as cheating.

00:36:31.000 --> 00:36:34.000
Piper: She wanted to validate that she deserved to be in that room

00:36:34.000 --> 00:36:36.000
Piper: in that seed, in that environment.

00:36:36.000 --> 00:36:39.000
Piper: And by no means, I actually think it's wonderful

00:36:39.000 --> 00:36:44.000
Piper: that part of an intern's onboarding for any type of job experience is including exposure to AI,

00:36:44.000 --> 00:36:49.000
Piper: to chat with colleagues, using it for workflows, etc. But this is a really interesting psychological

00:36:49.000 --> 00:36:55.000
Piper: barrier. One that I think is quite common, and again, 33% of women who are not using AI at work,

00:36:55.000 --> 00:37:02.000
Piper: it is because of that. It is because of that fear of being seen as cheating, as having this unfair

00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:08.000
Piper: advantage that somehow depreciates your own values and the value you bring.

00:37:08.000 --> 00:37:10.000
Piper: So this is quite eye-opening to me

00:37:10.000 --> 00:37:13.000
Piper: and I have started exploring this topic

00:37:13.000 --> 00:37:15.000
Piper: with a lot of female leaders.

00:37:15.000 --> 00:37:18.000
Piper: Female leaders, executives, executive vice presidents,

00:37:18.000 --> 00:37:22.000
Piper: chief revenue officers, CMOs, et cetera,

00:37:22.000 --> 00:37:24.000
Piper: who are all leaders that experience a lot of pressure

00:37:24.000 --> 00:37:27.000
Piper: to implement AI across the organization

00:37:27.000 --> 00:37:30.000
Piper: but observe a lot of fear and concerns and doubt

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:33.000
Piper: from their junior team and junior colleagues

00:37:33.000 --> 00:37:35.000
Piper: or even fellow managers and executives.

00:37:35.000 --> 00:37:36.000
Piper: And that's all reasonable.

00:37:36.000 --> 00:37:45.000
Piper: This is common.

00:37:36.000 --> 00:37:45.000
Piper: And I'm wanting to affirm that if you're experiencing that, this is absolutely the norm in this day and age.

00:37:45.000 --> 00:37:50.000
Piper: And there's a lot of interesting pedagogy around humans and change. We don't like change. We don't like changing habits.

00:37:50.000 --> 00:37:53.000
Piper: We're fearful of a lot of new technologies. It was the same with the internet. It was the same with television.

00:37:53.000 --> 00:37:57.000
Piper: It was the same with a lot of different technological advances.

00:37:57.000 --> 00:38:00.000
Piper: And so there's a whole line of research called change management.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:04.000
Piper: You work in tech or you work in sales. You will absolutely be familiar with this.

00:38:04.000 --> 00:38:09.000
Piper: And change management is essentially recognizing the social and cultural infrastructure that

00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:12.000
Piper: surrounds tool adoption.

00:38:12.000 --> 00:38:17.000
Piper: And you may have the best AI tool in the world, but if your team is terrified, and there's

00:38:17.000 --> 00:38:20.000
Piper: not a lot of psychological safety and they're worried about their jobs being replaced and,

00:38:20.000 --> 00:38:24.000
Piper: you know, having unstable income and, you know, it's an environment they feel unsettled

00:38:24.000 --> 00:38:28.000
Piper: in, like they will not adopt this amazing AI tool no matter how amazing it is.

00:38:28.000 --> 00:38:31.000
Piper: Now on the flip side, you could have a terrible AI tool,

00:38:31.000 --> 00:38:35.000
Piper: you could have terrible tech or SaaS in general,

00:38:35.000 --> 00:38:37.000
Piper: but if your team is so bought in,

00:38:37.000 --> 00:38:39.000
Piper: not just to the vision of the tool,

00:38:39.000 --> 00:38:40.000
Piper: but they have psychological safety,

00:38:40.000 --> 00:38:42.000
Piper: they're not worried about their jobs being taken over,

00:38:42.000 --> 00:38:45.000
Piper: they're seeing this as an asset to their workflows,

00:38:45.000 --> 00:38:46.000
Piper: it's become easier to use this tool

00:38:46.000 --> 00:38:49.000
Piper: than it was to do their previous workflow

00:38:49.000 --> 00:38:51.000
Piper: and they feel supported and safe

00:38:51.000 --> 00:38:52.000
Piper: and there's venues for them to be involved

00:38:52.000 --> 00:38:55.000
Piper: in the workflow design with this new tool,

00:38:55.000 --> 00:38:58.000
Piper: well, that tool can be wildly successful.

00:38:58.000 --> 00:39:01.000
Piper: So I wanna clarify that this AI challenge

00:39:01.000 --> 00:39:05.000
Piper: and adoption issue very often comes down to this social

00:39:05.000 --> 00:39:07.000
Piper: and, honestly, a leadership challenge

00:39:07.000 --> 00:39:10.000
Piper: and a management challenge around creating this environment

00:39:10.000 --> 00:39:14.000
Piper: for your team, your juniors, your organization to adopt.

00:39:14.000 --> 00:39:17.000
Piper: And part of that's not just getting them bought in

00:39:17.000 --> 00:39:20.000
Piper: to AI, yes, no, do you support AI, do you not?

00:39:20.000 --> 00:39:22.000
Piper: There's more nuance to it, but Leelock Voluk

00:39:22.000 --> 00:39:27.000
Piper: is a change management consultant based in the Middle East and I have found she has some

00:39:27.000 --> 00:39:34.000
Piper: really interesting perspectives on change management for AI about tactically building

00:39:34.000 --> 00:39:40.000
Piper: tactically building a plan that creates the psychological safety your team needs to feel

00:39:40.000 --> 00:39:44.000
Piper: confident and comfortable in this change and transition. Any of us any of us would be terrified

00:39:44.000 --> 00:39:50.000
Piper: if our bosses handed us a tool and said this is going to take over 50% of your job

00:39:50.000 --> 00:39:51.000
Piper: And that's it.

00:39:51.000 --> 00:39:55.000
Piper: Or they just hand you a tool that you realize it's going to take over 50% of your job.

00:39:55.000 --> 00:39:56.000
Piper: So it's reasonable.

00:39:56.000 --> 00:39:57.000
Piper: We are only humans.

00:39:57.000 --> 00:39:58.000
Piper: We are only mere mortals.

00:39:58.000 --> 00:40:02.000
Piper: Of course, we feel fear about our purpose and our value and our financial safety and

00:40:02.000 --> 00:40:03.000
Piper: future.

00:40:03.000 --> 00:40:07.000
Piper: And so the reason why gender adoption conversations are so important, it's not just about trying

00:40:07.000 --> 00:40:08.000
Piper: to uplift women.

00:40:08.000 --> 00:40:09.000
Piper: And that's very important to me.

00:40:09.000 --> 00:40:14.000
Piper: We need to spotlight more women who are grappling with these immense challenges, these

00:40:14.000 --> 00:40:21.000
Piper: very nuanced in the gray challenges of AI adoption, AI transformation and AI change management.

00:40:21.000 --> 00:40:27.000
Piper: But as we think about people of different backgrounds, people of different cultural heritages, of

00:40:27.000 --> 00:40:32.000
Piper: course there are differences in how we as individuals are upbringings our backgrounds,

00:40:32.000 --> 00:40:37.000
Piper: how we relate to AI, how we trust AI, how we feel at work and in these work environments

00:40:37.000 --> 00:40:42.000
Piper: and it's foolish to assume that discounting and ignoring those social dynamics and

00:40:42.000 --> 00:40:47.000
Piper: cultural dynamics in a work environment, it's foolish to assume ignoring them is

00:40:47.000 --> 00:40:52.000
Piper: still going to allow your AI implementation to be successful. And so

00:40:52.000 --> 00:40:55.000
Piper: there's a huge push right now, and this is what we do at Table Stakes Media, is

00:40:55.000 --> 00:40:59.000
Piper: we are trying to not only spotlight women's voices who are testing and

00:40:59.000 --> 00:41:02.000
Piper: experimenting and dappling in their personal lives but also for their teams

00:41:02.000 --> 00:41:05.000
Piper: in AI. We are trying to make AI skill building more accessible. We're trying

00:41:05.000 --> 00:41:12.000
Piper: of facility spaces where people can share the qualms and also talk about some of the discrepancies

00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:18.000
Piper: in their own work environments for psychological safety. And we can also want to sort of enlighten

00:41:18.000 --> 00:41:22.000
Piper: and share folks on different tactics to try and test out. I mean it's going to be different for

00:41:22.000 --> 00:41:27.000
Piper: every organization. There are a lot of different change management theories and concepts.

00:41:27.000 --> 00:41:33.000
Piper: One I found really interesting again from Leelock Bullock was this idea of creating office hours

00:41:33.000 --> 00:41:37.000
Piper: for your team to come to you to just share fears to listen support to listen

00:41:37.000 --> 00:41:39.000
Piper: You don't have to act on all of their concerns

00:41:39.000 --> 00:41:42.000
Piper: But to hear them to create space that creates psychological safety

00:41:42.000 --> 00:41:51.000
Piper: To have someone on your team that is knows the entire workflow to have them involved in this new AI workflow build as you're thinking in terms of systems and

00:41:51.000 --> 00:41:56.000
Piper: Where this tool can provide value and where it won't and how it gets implemented to include your team in on that process rather than just

00:41:56.000 --> 00:42:02.000
Piper: Saying hey, here's how it is, you know, take it or leave it and then also to think about a

00:42:02.000 --> 00:42:08.000
Piper: clear mapping of what tasks does this take over for your team members. If a

00:42:08.000 --> 00:42:14.000
Piper: app or tool is going to take over 50, 60, 70% of a teammate's, you know, job

00:42:14.000 --> 00:42:20.000
Piper: function, that's scary of course, but can you articulate that and say you're

00:42:20.000 --> 00:42:25.000
Piper: gonna have 70% more bandwidth and here are projects I want you to work on that

00:42:25.000 --> 00:42:26.000
Piper: I think your institutional knowledge, your expertise of our business, our

00:42:26.000 --> 00:42:29.000
Piper: or customers, et cetera, and our tech landscape

00:42:29.000 --> 00:42:31.000
Piper: and tech stack is going to help.

00:42:31.000 --> 00:42:35.000
Piper: And I only have 20% of projects for you

00:42:35.000 --> 00:42:37.000
Piper: to help fill your time.

00:42:37.000 --> 00:42:40.000
Piper: And you have 50% bandwidth left.

00:42:40.000 --> 00:42:42.000
Piper: And I want you to go take time to experiment

00:42:42.000 --> 00:42:44.000
Piper: in brainstorm ways that you could provide value.

00:42:44.000 --> 00:42:46.000
Piper: Different projects, things you're curious about,

00:42:46.000 --> 00:42:47.000
Piper: different things you've observed

00:42:47.000 --> 00:42:48.000
Piper: in your time at this company.

00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:51.000
Piper: And so give some agency and autonomy.

00:42:51.000 --> 00:42:54.000
Piper: Manage expectations that your team

00:42:54.000 --> 00:42:58.000
Piper: um, maybe has more bandwidth to experiment and double because what a dream scenario if

00:42:58.000 --> 00:43:03.000
Piper: your employees can come back to you and be like, hey, if this crazy new idea, and I actually

00:43:03.000 --> 00:43:08.000
Piper: think it can be a revenue driver, let me run this experiment, um, you know, let me build

00:43:08.000 --> 00:43:10.000
Piper: out this new campaign and we strategize and it's not their normal day-to-day job.

00:43:10.000 --> 00:43:15.000
Piper: But again, we can't discredit how expensive and costly it is to hire humans and how

00:43:15.000 --> 00:43:20.000
Piper: long it takes people to onboard and learn your business, your values, your mission,

00:43:20.000 --> 00:43:22.000
Piper: your vision, everything about your organization.

00:43:22.000 --> 00:43:29.000
Piper: And we can't discount the value of that could be applied to another team or department or initiative.

00:43:29.000 --> 00:43:32.000
Piper: So I think there's a lot of different tactics.

00:43:32.000 --> 00:43:34.000
Piper: There's a lot of wishful thinking and hopeful thinking.

00:43:34.000 --> 00:43:37.000
Piper: I do believe there's a world where we can support leaders better

00:43:37.000 --> 00:43:39.000
Piper: and give them more guidance and training,

00:43:39.000 --> 00:43:42.000
Piper: whether it's coaching or change management support,

00:43:42.000 --> 00:43:44.000
Piper: so that they can lead their teams.

00:43:44.000 --> 00:43:45.000
Piper: They can manage expectations.

00:43:45.000 --> 00:43:47.000
Piper: They can find ways of creating more value

00:43:47.000 --> 00:43:53.000
Piper: and allocating some of the more tactical tedious work towards AI tools and to

00:43:53.000 --> 00:43:57.000
Piper: help train and upskill this more junior workforce that can't follow the same

00:43:57.000 --> 00:44:00.000
Piper: career paths that we've had where you learn from tactics, you learn from the

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:04.000
Piper: doing, and then graduate to the strategy. They may have to jump right to the

00:44:04.000 --> 00:44:09.000
Piper: strategy or there may be other ways that we as leaders and mentors can help

00:44:09.000 --> 00:44:13.000
Piper: them transition to provide value for their careers but also for the

00:44:13.000 --> 00:44:15.000
Piper: the company, the team, the project, et cetera.

00:44:15.000 --> 00:44:18.000
Piper: So, you know, change management is incredibly important

00:44:18.000 --> 00:44:20.000
Piper: and you will absolutely observe,

00:44:20.000 --> 00:44:21.000
Piper: and by no means should you stereotype

00:44:21.000 --> 00:44:23.000
Piper: that the women on your team are gonna feel this way.

00:44:23.000 --> 00:44:26.000
Piper: But it's worth recognizing that there are discrepancies

00:44:26.000 --> 00:44:30.000
Piper: in trust, in inputs, in outputs,

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:32.000
Piper: in feeling psychological safety at work

00:44:32.000 --> 00:44:35.000
Piper: to test, to double experiment.

00:44:35.000 --> 00:44:36.000
Piper: Your team may fear making mistakes

00:44:36.000 --> 00:44:38.000
Piper: to worry that may cost their job.

00:44:38.000 --> 00:44:39.000
Piper: And if you're deep in the AI world,

00:44:39.000 --> 00:44:45.000
Piper: you know that a lot of AI building is constant quote-unquote mistakes. You're really just iterating

00:44:45.000 --> 00:44:50.000
Piper: and looking for things to troubleshoot and work and improve constantly. The product is not always

00:44:50.000 --> 00:44:56.000
Piper: done. You're always tinkering and iterating and that's a muscle and a cultural element that you

00:44:56.000 --> 00:45:01.000
Piper: may need to implement your team. You may need to foster that intentionally. So those are my

00:45:01.000 --> 00:45:06.000
Piper: thoughts around change management and I'll just shout out Table Six Media. We're building out a

00:45:06.000 --> 00:45:11.000
Piper: a podcast network and a talent network to help elevate the voices of women who are at the helm

00:45:11.000 --> 00:45:14.000
Piper: of this. They may not have all the answers but they're diving in, they're dabbling, they're

00:45:14.000 --> 00:45:21.000
Piper: leading major retailers and tech companies or I've even spoken to one woman who leads climate

00:45:21.000 --> 00:45:26.000
Piper: policy at the United Nations and she's also thinking about how does AI fit into her team

00:45:26.000 --> 00:45:29.000
Piper: and her landscape because her team is asking questions. They do want to know what that

00:45:29.000 --> 00:45:34.000
Piper: could look like. So they're women and men leading organizations and experimenting with this

00:45:34.000 --> 00:45:40.000
Piper: and I'm honored to be based in New York City and get to facilitate dinners and gatherings

00:45:40.000 --> 00:45:48.000
Piper: and we just did a AI content series with Shop Talk Fall by the Hyde Group and got to interview

00:45:48.000 --> 00:45:53.000
Piper: women specifically on the day to day of AI and where they're hopeful and where they have concerns.

00:45:53.000 --> 00:45:58.000
Piper: So I'm excited to publish that content and we have so much more amazing work in community

00:45:58.000 --> 00:46:06.000
Piper: and media and also in events and experiences to do to help progress this.

00:46:06.000 --> 00:46:16.000
Mira: What's the one bet you're making for the next 12 months that you cannot prove yet?

00:46:16.000 --> 00:46:24.000
Piper: Interesting. I don't know if... I don't know. The framing of this question is quite

00:46:24.000 --> 00:46:31.000
Piper: reductive to an experimentation mindset, which is that, you know, it either

00:46:31.000 --> 00:46:35.000
Piper: works or doesn't. And being a lifecycle marketer, you're constantly running AB

00:46:35.000 --> 00:46:39.000
Piper: tests, moving into an agentic decisioning tool where you're just

00:46:39.000 --> 00:46:42.000
Piper: trying to build infrastructure for constant testing and iterating.

00:46:42.000 --> 00:46:45.000
Piper: I think it's important to recognize we have a lot of hypotheses.

00:46:45.000 --> 00:46:46.000
Piper: We're humans.

00:46:46.000 --> 00:46:47.000
Piper: We have hypotheses.

00:46:47.000 --> 00:46:48.000
Piper: We have assumptions.

00:46:48.000 --> 00:46:51.000
Piper: You just have to hit the wall, you know, throw spaghetti at the wall and see what sticks

00:46:51.000 --> 00:46:55.000
Piper: and you have to run these tests very strategically, perhaps in an agile function.

00:46:55.000 --> 00:47:01.000
Piper: As I'm building table stakes media, we are absolutely betting on how career support, professional

00:47:01.000 --> 00:47:05.000
Piper: development support, change management support for leaders is going to trickle down and

00:47:05.000 --> 00:47:07.000
Piper: support their junior team members.

00:47:07.000 --> 00:47:12.000
