00:58:10.000 --> 00:58:13.000
Mira: saw those one-to-one micro experiments running live

00:58:13.000 --> 00:58:18.000
Mira: compared to the static logic systems you had built before?

00:58:18.000 --> 00:58:21.000
Piper: So it's quite a different way of thinking.

00:58:21.000 --> 00:58:26.000
Piper: I love to describe, and this is for listeners who obviously cannot see this visual, but I

00:58:26.000 --> 00:58:29.000
Piper: like users to envision kind of like a slot machine.

00:58:29.000 --> 00:58:35.000
Piper: Not only are these agents picking from a library of content, but imagine that your subject

00:58:35.000 --> 00:58:41.000
Piper: line has 50 variations of subject lines, and AMP and AI decision tools, but specifically

00:58:41.000 --> 00:58:47.000
Piper: Amp uses something quite unique called a semantic layer in which not only are the agents running

00:58:47.000 --> 00:58:54.000
Piper: these micro-experiments one-to-one, they're actually trying to learn. They're using reinforcement

00:58:54.000 --> 00:58:58.000
Piper: learning and they're tagging different components of a message. The subject line, the button,

00:58:58.000 --> 00:59:07.000
Piper: the hero image, the description, etc. They're almost componentizing these different elements

00:59:07.000 --> 00:59:12.000
Piper: of an email and SMS a push or even an in-app experience or in-website experience and they're

00:59:12.000 --> 00:59:15.000
Piper: trying to learn. They're trying to learn preferences. They're trying to observe how

00:59:15.000 --> 00:59:19.000
Piper: preferences change over time and some of those components can be things like a value proposition.

00:59:19.000 --> 00:59:23.000
Piper: This is sort of that core human desire, the thing that tongues at your heartstrings and

00:59:23.000 --> 00:59:28.000
Piper: many of us make assumptions and have a lot of institutional knowledge about our brands,

00:59:28.000 --> 00:59:32.000
Piper: about our companies to say, hey, most of our users love our product because they really

00:59:32.000 --> 00:59:40.000
Piper: value quality and well-made products and they also really value family and safety and those are all

00:59:40.000 --> 00:59:46.000
Piper: value propositions that align. Now candidly there may also be customers or potential buyers that

00:59:46.000 --> 00:59:52.000
Piper: care about sustainability or ethics or perhaps they're really motivated by affordability and

00:59:52.000 --> 00:59:57.000
Piper: and discounting. There's so many different types of value propositions when we worked with the

00:59:57.000 --> 01:00:00.000
Piper: National Sports Association. Some of the fans loved the drama. They loved the gossip. They loved the

01:00:00.000 --> 01:00:06.000
Mira (narration): What stayed with me is Piper saying the fear is not that the output is wrong but that using it will look like cheating, and that 33 percent of women who avoid the tools cite exactly that. The tension left open is how leaders create psychological safety fast enough for junior teams to stop protecting their own perceived value and start experimenting. My thanks to Piper Martz for mapping both the infrastructure shift and the social one without pretending either is solved. You can find her work at tablestakesmedia.com. The Nerra Network is at nerranetwork.com, where this show lives alongside Nerra Voices, and anyone with a story worth recording can apply to be a guest. I'm Mira. Until next time — the next voice could be yours.
